    faqs: [
        { title: 'What is the difference between TCP and UDP?', answer: 'TCP is a connection-oriented protocol providing reliable, ordered delivery with flow and congestion control. UDP is connectionless, offering no reliability guarantees but lower latency and overhead. TCP is used for web browsing and file transfers; UDP for DNS queries, streaming, and gaming.' },
        { title: 'How does a router differ from a switch?', answer: 'A switch operates at Layer 2, forwarding frames based on MAC addresses within a LAN. A router operates at Layer 3, forwarding packets based on IP addresses between networks. Routers make routing decisions using routing tables and protocols like OSPF and BGP.' },
        { title: 'What is NAT and why is it used?', answer: 'Network Address Translation maps private IP addresses to public IP addresses, allowing multiple devices on a private network to share a single public IP for internet access. NAT conserves IPv4 addresses and provides a layer of security by hiding internal network structure.' },
        { title: 'How does DNS resolution work?', answer: 'When you type a URL, your browser checks its cache, then the OS resolver cache, then sends a query to a recursive DNS resolver. The resolver queries root servers, TLD servers, and authoritative servers in sequence to obtain the IP address. Results are cached at each level according to TTL values.' },
        { title: 'What is the purpose of subnetting?', answer: 'Subnetting divides a larger network into smaller, manageable segments. Benefits include reduced broadcast domains, improved security through isolation, efficient IP address allocation, and easier network management. Subnet masks determine which portion of an IP address identifies the network vs. host.' },
        { title: 'What is CIDR notation?', answer: 'CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) notation expresses IP addresses with their prefix length, e.g., 192.168.1.0/24. The number after the slash indicates how many bits form the network portion. /24 means 24 network bits and 8 host bits, allowing 254 usable host addresses.' },
        { title: 'How does TLS secure web traffic?', answer: 'TLS performs a handshake where client and server agree on a cipher suite, the server presents a certificate for identity verification, and both parties derive shared session keys. Subsequent data is encrypted symmetrically. TLS provides confidentiality, integrity, and server authentication.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between a hub, switch, and router?', answer: 'A hub broadcasts all traffic to every port (Layer 1). A switch learns MAC addresses and forwards frames only to the appropriate port (Layer 2). A router forwards packets between different networks using IP addresses (Layer 3). Each operates at a higher layer with more intelligence.' },
        { title: 'How do VLANs work?', answer: 'VLANs (Virtual LANs) logically segment a physical switch into separate broadcast domains. Ports are assigned to VLANs; traffic between VLANs requires a router (inter-VLAN routing). 802.1Q tagging allows VLAN traffic to traverse trunk links between switches while maintaining isolation.' },
        { title: 'What is the purpose of ARP?', answer: 'ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) maps IP addresses to MAC addresses on a local network. When a host needs to send to an IP on its subnet, it broadcasts an ARP request. The target responds with its MAC address. Results are cached in the ARP table to avoid repeated broadcasts.' },
        { title: 'How does OSPF differ from RIP?', answer: 'OSPF is a link-state protocol that builds a complete topology map and uses Dijkstra\'s algorithm. RIP is a distance-vector protocol using hop count (max 15). OSPF converges faster, supports VLSM, hierarchical areas, and scales to larger networks. RIP is simpler but limited in scale and metric flexibility.' },
        { title: 'What is BGP and why is it important?', answer: 'BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) is the inter-domain routing protocol that connects autonomous systems on the internet. It uses path-vector routing with policy-based decisions. BGP is critical because it determines how traffic flows between ISPs, cloud providers, and enterprise networks globally.' },
        { title: 'What is SDN and how does it change networking?', answer: 'Software-Defined Networking separates the control plane from the data plane, centralizing network intelligence in a controller. This enables programmable network management, faster provisioning, consistent policies, and easier automation compared to configuring individual devices with vendor-specific CLIs.' },
        { title: 'How does HTTP/2 improve over HTTP/1.1?', answer: 'HTTP/2 uses binary framing instead of text, multiplexes multiple requests over a single TCP connection (eliminating head-of-line blocking at the HTTP layer), compresses headers with HPACK, supports server push, and allows stream prioritization. These features significantly improve page load performance.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between WPA2 and WPA3?', answer: 'WPA3 replaces WPA2\'s PSK handshake with SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals), providing forward secrecy so captured traffic cannot be decrypted later. WPA3 also protects against offline dictionary attacks, offers 192-bit security for enterprise mode, and simplifies IoT device onboarding.' },
        { title: 'How does a CDN improve web performance?', answer: 'A CDN caches content at edge servers close to end users, reducing latency by serving requests from the nearest location. It offloads traffic from origin servers, absorbs traffic spikes, and improves availability. CDNs also provide DDoS mitigation and TLS termination at the edge.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between IPv4 and IPv6?', answer: 'IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses (4.3 billion addresses), while IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses (virtually unlimited). IPv6 simplifies headers, eliminates NAT by providing end-to-end addressing, includes built-in IPsec support, and supports stateless autoconfiguration. IPv6 does not broadcast; it uses multicast instead.' },
        { title: 'How does DHCP assign IP addresses?', answer: 'DHCP uses a four-step process: Discover (client broadcasts to find servers), Offer (server proposes an address), Request (client accepts an offer), Acknowledge (server confirms the lease). The client also receives subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers, and lease duration.' },
        { title: 'What is the purpose of ICMP?', answer: 'ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol) provides error reporting and diagnostic functions for IP. Key messages include Echo Request/Reply (used by ping), Destination Unreachable (when a host or port cannot be reached), Time Exceeded (TTL expired, used by traceroute), and Redirect (suggesting a better route).' },
        { title: 'What is a VLAN trunk and how does 802.1Q tagging work?', answer: 'A trunk link carries traffic for multiple VLANs between switches. 802.1Q inserts a 4-byte tag into the Ethernet frame containing a VLAN ID (1-4094). The receiving switch reads the tag to determine which VLAN the frame belongs to. The native VLAN sends frames untagged on the trunk.' },
    ],
